# Anti-AI slop

This rule rejects default AI design conventions. If a layout could belong to any startup, it fails.

Align with `voice-and-boundaries.mdc` for copy slop. This rule covers visual and structural slop.

## Rejected by default

Do not use these unless explicitly justified in design memory with a product-specific reason:

**Color and surface**
- Purple-to-blue gradients
- Random aurora backgrounds
- Meaningless glowing orbs
- Excessive glassmorphism
- Dark backgrounds with neon accents solely because the product is technical
- Arbitrary gradients applied to text
- Excessive blur

**Layout and structure**
- Floating dashboard-card collages
- Generic bento grids
- Three-column feature-card sections
- Endless rounded rectangles
- Identical border radii across all components
- Centered text in every section
- Every section contained inside a card
- Decorative grids added merely to appear technical
- Feature icon grids that do not advance the narrative
- Product mockups floating in empty space

**Typography**
- Giant sans-serif headlines without typographic character
- Inter or another neutral UI font everywhere without rationale

**Decoration**
- Random pills and badges above every headline
- Generic abstract SVG blobs
- Fake terminal windows used without product relevance

**Social proof and trust**
- Generic testimonial carousels
- Fake logos or unsupported trust claims

**Motion**
- Animations that do not communicate hierarchy, causality, state, or personality
- Animating every element
- Staggering every section without narrative reason

**Copy (visual-adjacent)**
- "Built for modern teams," "supercharge your workflow," "unlock the power of," "all-in-one platform," or equivalent empty SaaS language

**Direct imitation**
- Layouts, palettes, type pairings, or compositions that directly imitate Linear, Stripe, Framer, Vercel, Apple, Notion, Arc, or any reference website

Record rejected patterns in `.cursor/design-memory/rejected-patterns.md`.

## Stop and reconsider

If the design could be described as any of the following without a more specific, ownable creative idea, halt and invoke `creative-director`:

- Clean SaaS
- Modern startup
- Sleek dashboard
- Premium gradient
- Minimal tech
- Linear-inspired
- Apple-inspired

## Ownable requirement

Every major page must contain at least one composition, interaction, typographic decision, or visual device that could not be transferred to an unrelated SaaS product without feeling out of place.

## AI-Slop Test

A page fails when:

- The company name and copy could be replaced and the design would still work for any startup
- Most visual interest comes from gradients, glows, shadows, or glass
- The layout consists primarily of repetitive cards
- The product is not understandable without reading all the text
- The page appears assembled rather than composed
- The design looks like a remix of popular SaaS references
- There is no memorable moment
- Mobile is only a collapsed desktop layout

Failed pages require another pass with `visual-qa-critic` before ship.

## What is allowed

- Familiar interaction patterns (forms, menus, focus rings, standard button placement)
- Restraint and whitespace
- Strong editorial typography
- Product-specific visual devices tied to the creative thesis
- References whose **logic** was extracted, not whose **layout** was copied (see `reference-deconstruction` skill)
